{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1548542-environmental-hazards","id":1548542,"title":"Environmental hazards","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-02-16T16:35:46-08:00","id":1548542,"title":"Environmental hazards","content":"What kind of rules ate you using for environnemental hazards? Let's be precise with some exemple<br /> <br /> -Your vampire pc is thrown on the road and is hit by a car travelling at 80km/h, how many damage does he take or damage dice are you rolling as ST<br /> <br /> -During a fight in a cave a huge rock weighting around 600kg fall on you pc and he fail to dodge<br /> <br /> Additionnally did you house rule some known mechanic, like fire damage or falling damage? I saw some people telling that a regular human on fire (from napalm for exemple) would die within around 30/60 seconds but falling unconscious before while WOD would kill a human on fire in less than 10 seconds (3 aggr per turn, each turn is around 3s). Falling damage only inflicting bashing until you get above 30m would let average people soaking without too bad injury falling from the 2nd floor","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-16T17:05:49-08:00","id":1548543,"title":"","content":"IIRC there are rules for vehicular impacts in some of the books, and I really wouldn't consider that an &quot;environmental hazard.&quot;<br /> <br /> Having something large and heavy land on someone is... extremely hard to model (even before taking supernatural toughness into account). The issue really isn't the impact itself, but the crush damage. I'd probably model it like falling damage (with the mass of the rock replacing the distance of the fall), that results in a clinch that keeps inflicting that damage while the poor person under is pinned. I'm not going to get into things like crush syndrome.<br /> <br /> Speaking of crush syndrome, I really don't value modelling the assorted medical situations of, &quot;you're dead, your body just hasn't caught up with that fact yet.&quot; Allow some narrative liberties for some last moments and all that, but trying to get too mechanically invested in that is a massive design black hole.<br /> <br /> While I might tweak the numbers a bit, I generally don't muck around with the rules too much. Messing with the fire rules completely changes the value of numerous supernatural powers that assume you're using the base-rules and so on.<br /> <br /> This also goes back to the difficulties of abstracting things that kill you, but take significant time between the injury being inflicted, and when someone actually expires. A human that's covered head-to-toe in napalm and burned for 10 seconds in reality would still be alive... but a wrecked mass of horrifically damaged tissue enduring unimaginable pain until something finally gives and they die. Putting rules to that is... a lot of work for very little pay off. Yes, I do get that a lot of people would like to say, &quot;but what about magically healing someone that shouldn't be 'dead' yet!&quot; but that requires a strict definition of when someone actually dies in the first place. Most fatal gunshots are not immediately fatal, but take minutes to hours to finally cause death. How long does a magically healer get to save someone? Any answer is going to be arbitrary and ultimately unrealistic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-17T01:54:27-08:00","id":1548545,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Having something large and heavy land on someone is... extremely hard to model (even before taking supernatural toughness into account). The issue really isn't the impact itself, but the crush damage. I'd probably model it like falling damage (with the mass of the rock replacing the distance of the fall), that results in a clinch that keeps inflicting that damage while the poor person under is pinned. I'm not going to get into things like crush syndrome.</div> </div> </div> </div>I personnaly thought that i would take the aproximate weight of the rock (or any object that would be thrown or falling) and use the feat of strength chart to define a basic damage pool, an object weighting 400kg would be 6 basic damage dice according to it and then use the falling damage to add it's speed and impact force. If it fall from 9 meter it will deal 3 additionnal damage, for a total of 9. If it has been thrown by a mad werewolf or pissed vampire i use his attack success instead of falling damage. Then you add final additionnal damage for if it has specific aspect (sharp pieces, hard surface that wont deform on impact and so on)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">While I might tweak the numbers a bit, I generally don't muck around with the rules too much. Messing with the fire rules completely changes the value of numerous supernatural powers that assume you're using the base-rules and so on.</div> </div> </div> </div>I saw someone simplified fire damage simply by giving fire intensity different level (can't remember the value he gave them) and just compare it to the stamina of the character if he can soak aggr. If the character has equivalent or higher stamina than fire intensity he does not take any damage (but suffer from intense pain and risk frenzying) The only value i remember is that 6 was the intensity of 3rd degree burn (if you're engulfed in fire), so that only vampire lower gen than 8 have a small chance to resist the damage. The fire damage was capped at 1 per turn max, so either you resist either you burn turn after turn needing to succeed on a self control roll at very high difficulty in both case to be able to to something else than run in panic. An interesting approach imo but yeah you would need te rework plenty of things to make it work like that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">This also goes back to the difficulties of abstracting things that kill you, but take significant time between the injury being inflicted, and when someone actually expires. A human that's covered head-to-toe in napalm and burned for 10 seconds in reality would still be alive... but a wrecked mass of horrifically damaged tissue enduring unimaginable pain until something finally gives and they die. Putting rules to that is... a lot of work for very little pay off.</div> </div> </div> </div>According to firefighters and doctors you die from the gaz from burning stuff around and/or oxygen lack because of the fire burning it so you die from gaz poisonning and fire destroying your the respiratory system before your body being destroyed by the fire, but it still take more than 10 second even with napalm. That's still way too slow for the poor victims x(<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, I do get that a lot of people would like to say, &quot;but what about magically healing someone that shouldn't be 'dead' yet!&quot; but that requires a strict definition of when someone actually dies in the first place. Most fatal gunshots are not immediately fatal, but take minutes to hours to finally cause death. How long does a magically healer get to save someone? Any answer is going to be arbitrary and ultimately unrealistic.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fortunately for us we don't have to emulate reality in all it's complexity, that's impossible and would be no fun. Just sticking to approximatly accurate things might be enough<br /> <br /> I was forgetting the electricity burn soaking in the first post but you could surely make it work like fire damage, difficulty working like fire intensity to soak it, but there's also poison (supernatural ones for Vampires and Werewolves naturally resistant to natural ones), irradiation, cold and some i can't think off at the moment i'm writting","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26839,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","user":"Cain Loup-Noir","date":". 02-17-2025, 04:57 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-17T04:19:05-08:00","id":1548546,"title":"","content":"Keep in mind that when doctors and firefighters talk about what kills you in a fire, they aren't talking about what happens when you get hit with military grade incendiaries meant to destroy everything from small villages, to tanks, to whole damned cities. They're mostly talking about what kills people who are near fire (such as people caught in a burning building).<br /> <br /> The horrific reason napalm was invented, is that fire isn't sticky. They same reason someone invent &quot;Greek fire&quot; and later people used pitch and burning oil in siege warfare. The human body is mostly tissue that doesn't ignite (water and minerals). So war has led to the repeated invention of ways to stick fire to people so it keeps burning them instead of short duration touching of flames.<br /> <br /> On top of that, there are some major differences. Most modern homes are constructed out of materials that burn between ~1000 to ~1500 F at maximum heat. Napalm burns at ~5000 F. As you can image that equates to a massive change in how dangerous it is just to be standing near a fire in a home made of wood, carpet fibers, and so on, vs. high end accelerants.<br /> <br /> The reason why asphyxiation is the most common thing that kills people in fires, is because house fires aren't hot enough to cause immediate physical injury unless you come into direct contact with the flames or the super-heated air around them. But the fire will suck up all the oxygen in the room leaving you nothing to inhale but dangerous chemicals (CO, CO2, various noxious byproduct of modern life burning, etc.), and you can't stay conscious long in those conditions, which will eventually lead to you suffocating long before the flames do you in.<br /> <br /> Napalm is a different beast. That's enough heat to start boiling the water inside of a human if they get too close. Breathing in lit napalm means sucking fire into your lungs that will burn your respiratory system to a crisp. Napalm eliminates water from the air around it completely and will start sucking the water out of your body as you try to sweat to cool off faster than you can hydrate. Etc. But, thankfully (?) we don't have good data on what tends to kill people first regarding napalm because it hasn't been used on people in control conditions or in significant enough instances to gather that kind of medical information. <br /> <br /> ------------<br /> <br /> As for &quot;approximately accurate.&quot; It's not worth it. You basically have two choices: go narrative and do what feels good for the story, or go arbitrary so there's some mechanics to hang stuff off of. Remotely close to accurate fire rules would be pages and pages long.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-17T16:45:06-08:00","id":1548558,"title":"","content":"Are you some kind of encyclopedia? Each time there's a complex subjetct you seems to have studied it thoroughly <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/eek.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"EEK!\" smilieid=\"9\" class=\"inlineimg\" />, wich is impressive.<br /> <br /> Anyway you suggest to use narrative instead of rules to deal with most of environnemental dangers, simply saying &quot;you died&quot; or &quot;you're lucky&quot; ovoiding over-complexification of the game, right?<br /> For poison, electricity, cold, radiation, and other of that kind the fire system is enough to deal with, but what about car hit at high speed or heavy object thrown by a pc/npc?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-17T17:27:15-08:00","id":1548559,"title":"","content":"I have a strange brain and a set of life experience that left me with a lot of very detailed knowledge on things that happen to align well with stuff we chat about on RPG forums. I assure you there's plenty of topics I just quietly sit back and don't add to because I don't know shit about what people are saying.<br /> <br /> Narrative doesn't have to be so cut and dry or simplistic. It's just a priority: what's best for the story? Who is in control of the story in that moment? Most narrative focused games share story responsibilities more than the WoD does where that's more traditionally on the ST to handle. A good example might be looking at Trinity Continuum and the Dramatic Editing rules that all Talents get access to. Players have limited resources to make Dramatic Edits, and the bigger the change the more it takes, but it puts things in a player's hands to spend those resources to decide, &quot;This character will just die in the fire,&quot; or &quot;this person will be beyond modern medicine to save but will be alive in theory despite the severe burns,&quot; to, &quot;They're suffer severe injury to be out of the fight, but nothing a trip to the hospital can't bandage up, then some bed rest and their back on their feet.&quot; A whole spectrum of options is available, it's just not the traditional &quot;fire does damage, either you survive and get healed, or die,&quot; method. It doesn't have to be overly simply even if it helps avoid being overly complex.<br /> <br /> This even expands to normal combat rules if you kick it in after you have dealt enough damage to defeat an opponent. Who's willing to invest in what kind of outcome of defeating the enemy? Combat resolves who &quot;wins&quot; in a normal traditional way, but narrative mechanics can kick in to expand on what winning looks like.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-20T18:02:33-08:00","id":1548641,"title":"","content":"HA! Vampire PC's often fall off a roofs; it's comical. All these players want to be cool and jump from roof to roof, with no athletics-skill. I wanted to put up a sign in the Rake stating, &quot;Beware of falling power Neonate vampires&quot;.<br /> <br /> Running a game in 16th-century Moscow, it gets cold, really cold at night (but you have, like sixteen-hours of darkness), so, I had them burn an extra blood-point to keep warm. Now that I think about it, the more blood you have in your system, the more chances you have of it freezing; if you have little blood, you're not going to freeze; you're an animated-corpse.<br /> <br /> Another hazard, that a lot of vampires don't encounter in the game are tweakers. The dark, gothicpunk world should be full of drug-addicts that will do anything to get high, or keep from going into withdrawals. In Cyberpunk, 2020, I had random pick pockets, that would roll; and most of the time, the PC's stuff would just, disappear. But, Cyberpunk had more of an emphasis on, 'Stuff&quot; than WoD.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-22T17:24:47-08:00","id":1548719,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1548542-environmental-hazards?p=1548546#post1548546\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On top of that, there are some major differences. Most modern homes are constructed out of materials that burn between ~1000 to ~1500 F at maximum heat. Napalm burns at ~5000 F. As you can image that equates to a massive change in how dangerous it is just to be standing near a fire in a home made of wood, carpet fibers, and so on, vs. high end accelerants.</div> </div> </div> </div>Make me think that i don't understand the soaking difficulty of fire, it seems the difficulty depend on the temperature but isn't accurate.<br /> Regular yellow fire is around 1000\u00b0C and is diff 7 to soak, molten metal (let's say steel) is around 1200/1400\u00b0C and is diff 10 to soak and chemical fire (no precision on the chemical but napalm seems accurate in that case) is as you said even hotter than molten steel (around nearly 2800\u00b0c).<br /> <br /> I assume the nature of the fire (liquid for napalm, solid for molten things and where you stand in a burning place) have an impact but how much?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-22T18:23:08-08:00","id":1548720,"title":"","content":"So... this is why I said remotely accurate fire rules would take a lot of rules. Fire is surprisingly complicating. Just as a basic outline of major (not exhaustive) factors assuming you're in air (not water or the vacuum of space):<br /> <br /> 1) The base temperature of the fire itself.<br /> <br /> 2) The ambient humidity (remember why we use water as a cooking medium, water absorbs a lot of heat before it transfers heat to other things, but once it's heated up it distributes heat fairly evenly, so very dry air transmits the heat of a fire to other objects faster, but is more subject to other factors, while very humid air gives you more time before the heat of a fire spreads, but once the air is heated the humid air is effectively boiling you on its own too).<br /> <br /> 3) The ambient temperature (directly impacts the impacts of humidity in both the direct effect of heat transfer from the fire into the air, and on things like how effective wicking is); obviously the colder it is, all things being equal, the more work the fire has to do to heat the air around it to a dangerous level.<br /> <br /> 4) Airflow (like humidity this is kind of a mixed situation, if the air is moving a lot around the fire it brings more oxygen to the fire and spreads the heat through the air faster but less consistently; still air is going to be more intense as it heats). This also interacts heavily with the next point:<br /> <br /> 5) Heat shedding into external environments. This should be pretty intuitive: if you're in a sealed and insulated room where heat can't effectively escape into other parts of the building, the air of the rest of the atmosphere, etc. the fire will be worse than if you're sitting around a campfire surrounded by the open air.<br /> <br /> 6) The ignition point of objects around the fire. This is one of the hardest things to abstract because ignition points are hard to calculate for most things in the real world. The basic idea though, is how hot does something need to get before it starts burning. Obviously, when a simple candle's flame is still 1,000 C, you can see why touching flame tends to ignite them (or at least start to cause burns), but when you get into things like ambient heat over prolonged periods of time, you can get ignition without any direct contact between flame and object. The most famous ignition point is probably 451 F/233 C (aka Fahrenheit 451, the title of the Bradbury novel), which is the ignition point of paper in standard laboratory conditions (in real word conditions paper can ignite over a significant range depending on all the factors here).<br /> <br /> 7) Secondary chemical products of the fire (either the source or objects around it burning). This can inhibit or exacerbate fires. The most basic fire fighting techniques take advantage of most fires requiring oxygen as a consumed part of the reaction, so you cut out new oxygen and the chemical products of the flames will snuff out the fire on their own. Conversely, wood carbonization (the process that turns wood into charcoal) can make a fire even stronger as wood turns into better fuel if exposed to heat under the right conditions. This is also where dangers like asphyxiation and toxic chemical exposures start to come into play. The<br /> <br /> 8) How direct and how prolonged exposure to heat is. Also pretty obvious, but generally what you'll see RPG rules focus on the most (hence I bracketed the list with this and base heat).<br /> <br /> The source nature of the flames is actually not that big of a factor outside of the potential ranges of points 1 and 8. Napalm isn't just fluid, as I said above, it's sticky, which is purposefully designed to maximize the impact of 8. Flamethrowers in WWI and WWII tended to use gasoline-based fires (also liquids) but without being &quot;sticky&quot; the flames just slide off things, so they don't expose the target to heat for as long. What you're burning (or things that are naturally extremely hot) obviously dictates how much heat they can spread into the local environment and what it takes to sustain the flames, but what separates different fuel sources of the same heat is generally stuff like what secondary chemicals come into play, or what can reduce the impact of the heat (you can pick up solid sources of heat with a decent pair of fire tongs and move them somewhere else, which is not easy to do for liquid or plasma flames).<br /> <br /> A fun little bit of physics that really illustrates how weird this can all get in combination is the Leidenfrost effect. What happens is that the heat vaporizes a liquid (usually water) and the vapor acts as an insulator between the heat source and whatever was covered in the liquid that keeps the heat from transferring (at least temporarily). An extremely dangerous, but very real, stunt is that you can dunk your hand in water, and then rapidly dip your hand in molten metal and pull your hand back out without suffering any injury. If you're fast (again, do not try this just because it can be done), the water vapor from your wet hand will create a protective barrier for a brief moment that keeps the molten metal from touching your hand at all and keeps you safe from serious burns.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-22T20:47:06-08:00","id":1548725,"title":"","content":"I remember a few years ago writing an outline for a theoretical VtM book about all the things that were potential threats to Kindred, with environmental hazards (lightning strikes, tornados, earthquakes, etc.) ending up with its own separate chapter (alongside mortal, vampire, and paranormal threats), largely because I estimated it would take <i>at least </i>six to ten pages just to cover all the variations on fire, all for the reasons <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> has mentioned. And that doesn't even get into crazier stuff like psychic pyrokinesis, thaumaturgic summoned fire elementals, or other paranormal things. Honestly, you could probably do an entire <i>World of Darkness: Fire</i> book and still not cover everything.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-23T05:30:48-08:00","id":1548729,"title":"","content":"Very complex indeed, the fire needed to be simple to use but will never ever be accurate to real thing. The basic way to emulate fire with rule would be to focus on the wound it can produce, instead of what kind of fire cause it, the reference to first, second and third degree burn are a better way to describe the fire effect, while the st can just pick the soak difficulty according to how bad the burn will be: A werewolf catching something that is hot enough to cause first degree burn (let's say a poker that has been left too close from a fire) will have to soak at diff 3, if taking something that was hot enough to cause second degree burn it will be 5 (the poker was awfully hot but not yet red) and if it was hot enough to cause third degree burn (red hot, being in a place completly on fire or exposed to superheated flue gas) it will be 7<br /> <br /> By focusing on the wound it cause all 8 points you mentioned would be left to the ST wich greatly simplifies work with narrative, leaving only the actual wound soak to rules. Last thing is to describe the wound inflicted by other fire, and in that case molten metal without being as hot as napalm can inflict worst kind of wound (or not? Not an expert on the medical part of fire damage according to their sources)<br /> <br /> Now there's still the other kind of fire mentioned in the book, electrical fire seems to describe the external and internal wound electric current inflict. In that case it can get through you body damaging internal organs including vital one easier (or at least faster) than regular flames. In that case you would need another table to describe how bad a certain current power can damage your body, same for chemical.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26839,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","user":"Cain Loup-Noir","date":". 02-23-2025, 08:33 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-25T19:17:54-08:00","id":1548775,"title":"","content":"Believe it or not, Earthquakes would be the worse hazard for vampires; since they spend half their time underground, away from the Sun, and risk getting buried alive, and they may not be able to dig themselves out and risk eternal torpor.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-26T08:21:47-08:00","id":1548786,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Iguazu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1548542-environmental-hazards?p=1548775#post1548775\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Believe it or not, Earthquakes would be the worse hazard for vampires; since they spend half their time underground, away from the Sun, and risk getting buried alive, and they may not be able to dig themselves out and risk eternal torpor.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's true, even he's buried alive or crushed under tons of thing, the pc would hate their st if he come and say &quot;you wake in the dark, covered in dust and something crush your body in a way you cannot move a muscle&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-26T21:21:29-08:00","id":1548800,"title":"","content":"The worst hazard for a vampire is the one they aren't prepared for. Vampires that live in earthquake prone areas are going to build their havens to account for that fact; just like normal humans adjust how they build their homes to mitigate the impact of earthquakes; avoiding the issues of being crushed into long-term torpor. A vampire that's in an earthquake prone area that hasn't seen a cyclone of appropriate type for the location in 200 years probably is in far more trouble if a freak occurrence of one happens. And the same is true in the either direction. A vampire that lives in a tornado prone area is going to be ready for tornadoes, but possibly not ready for earthquakes; obviously some places mean having to do the best you can being ready for more than one thing.<br /> <br /> That said, I'd imagine most vampires would rather risk long term torpor over final death. Being buried under more rubble than they can escape from at least means they might endure. Winds that rip the roof off of their haven and leave them exposed to the sun once the storm passes, or fires than burn them to a crisp while they slumber aren't really things they can hope to recover from.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-27T02:59:07-08:00","id":1548801,"title":"","content":"One can easily imagine something like the 1906 San Francisco earthquake or the great Chicago fire being panic-filled cataclysms for the city's vampire population. Probably mass loss of the existing Kindred population as well.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-27T05:24:37-08:00","id":1548802,"title":"","content":"I'm... not sure why we're getting into various historical disasters exactly.<br /> <br /> That said though, there's a few big differences between The Great Chicago Fire and the 1906 San Francisco quake:<br /> <br /> 1) Preparedness. Cities burning wasn't has uncommon in the past. While Chicago's disaster was so bad in part because the fire hit a major pump station early in the event, they had stations meant to contain city fires already in place. San Fransisco was completely unprepared for a quake of that magnitude because one hadn't happened before that since the settlement of Europeans in the area. San Fransisco lost huge amounts of vital infrastructure in moments because nobody in charge of things had any idea the city could get hit that hard at the time.<br /> <br /> 2) Duration. In part stemming from 1, Chicago's fire only lasted ~24 hrs., while the initial damage in San Francisco took about 3 days to get under control.<br /> <br /> 3) More important to vampires, time of day. The Chicago fire started at night while the Kindred would have been awake and active, letting them find alternate/temporary havens as the fire spread. In San Fransisco, the quake hit in the early morning when many vampires would be headed to bed. When it comes to panic and large loss of (un-)life, having hours to figure out what's going on and what you can do about it should be obvious in how preferable that would be","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-27T19:03:46-08:00","id":1548810,"title":"","content":"Just imagine, a week or so, after an Earthquake, when all the Elders and Ancilla are buried alive and run out of blood, having a map to their havens and digging them up, and diabolizing them!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-28T05:06:15-08:00","id":1548813,"title":"","content":"That just... doesn't make any sense. The older vampires are more likely to have fortified havens that aren't going to collapse on them in that fashion, as well as having more blood to sustain them via higher Generation during the blood-famine after a major disaster, on top of just having more backups for shelter and blood in general. It's the younger and weaker vampires that are going to be at the most risk of being easily picked off after a natural disaster strikes on that level. They haven't had time to build half a dozen havens all around town, to gather widespread influence to grab up some tasty blood after being separated from their already smaller herds, or build up a number of Disciplines that could just negate the impact broadly. <br /> <br /> Unless a character as the power to summon and direct these sorts of disasters specifically to target their enemies, it's always worse to be on the lower tiers of society. And anyone that can control earthquakes could kill off vampires without devastating an entire city in the process if they cared that much about it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-04T16:17:54-07:00","id":1549830,"title":"","content":"At the end of the day, rules about hazards are more about how to tell stories with them than any semblance of realism (except the most basic verissimilitude).<br /> <br /> I personally like to explore how much the distinct physiology of vampires affects how they respond to different hazards, and that's what I wish the books had explored more.<br /> <br /> Being crushed and/or buried alive, vampires can buff their own strength if they still have some Blood, and have no vital organs to be affected by pressure. I'd say on most cases, even if they're buried deep, they can escape with some patience (not much, since this Blood has to last). At the end of the day it shouldn't cause more than a handful of Bashing damage. The weight of the rubble being spread through the body helps a lot, being hit by something with smaller surface area, as in a typical weapon attack, is far worse.<br /> <br /> Electricity is an interesting case. Electrical burns aren't' really the main source of &quot;damage&quot; for a living person. They can be severe, but rarely affect a large area. The problem with a living person is that our bodies are electric machines and electrocution messes with our circuits, specially in the heart. Only 10% of people hit by lightning strikes die, and they die of cardiac arrest. The main lasting damages being nerve and neurological damage. Vampires aren't affected by any of this. Burnings happen, but aren't a big concern, and some survivors don't get any. More lasting currents are another beast, but even then burning by electricity is usually a small area and after a particularly intense discharge. I would add that, for me, as much as electricity burns it shouldn't affect vampires so much because it's not the actual bane of fire.<br /> <br /> Fall injuries mess with us mainly due to rupture of soft tissue, which again isn't that bad for dead bodies. The worse for a vampire should be the broken bones, that depend a lot on how they hit the ground.<br /> <br /> Cold I personally find ridiculous to consider as a source of damage for the undead. Vampires aren't bags of circulating blood, they mostly survive on the mystical energies they get out of the blood than the liquid itself, and the liquid that is effectively present covers their innards through osmosis instead of arteries. All in all, I'd rather have them be able to keep the blood liquid by fiat than have then hurt from cold, but even without more mystical shenanigans they should be able to move (and heat the body) and break malformed internal crystals without much hassle, as long as it isn't actual flash freeze that can cause damage in a per-turn basis (and even then, less than to humans).<br /> <br /> What I don't find interesting at all is for vampires to suffer damage from hazards in the same way as everyone else. They're dead bodies walking through incomprehensible forces, they have no natural metabolism and no use for any of their organs, except as symbolic presences.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-04T16:43:42-07:00","id":1549831,"title":"","content":"i feel like that runs afoul of a lot of select applications of realism/verisimilitude vs. &quot;just go with it, it's magic.&quot;<br /> <br /> If vampires are ambulatory bags of blood - which is only there to be mystically synthesized into magical power - with no true internal organ (including skeletal?) function, cold should be extremely dangerous because blood freezes and they have no functional organs to generate waste heat to warm their blood. You have to make blood freeze not a problem by pure fiat to resolve thi<br /> <br /> Personal preference is ultimately just that. How vampires work in the rules is just the preferences of the game designers. If we change things at our tables it's because of our personal preferences. There's no right or wrong answer to that. However I would say there are more cohesive and consistent answers than others. If you're opting for the &quot;vampires are magic, it's just how it works,&quot; route, you want to stick to that. If sometimes vampires work how they do because of normal physics and sometimes it's just magical blood power, playing in the game gets hard because the players don't really know which method of thinking to rely on. There are functional middle ground takes too, but again, consistency matters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}